<Base title="Bespoke Software or Off-the-Shelf: Where to Start" description="A frustrating spreadsheet isn’t always a reason to build an application. First, understand the work it is doing."><article class="prose wrap"><a class="eyebrow" href="/insights">INSIGHTS / PRACTICAL THINKING</a><h1>Do you need bespoke software—or a better setup?</h1><p class="article-lead">A frustrating spreadsheet isn’t always a reason to build an application. First, understand the work it is doing.</p><h2>Map one workflow</h2><p>Choose a process that happens often: receiving stock, assigning a job or preparing a client report. Write down what starts it, who takes each step, which information moves between people and where delays occur.</p><h2>Check what you already have</h2><p>An existing tool may support the workflow with better configuration. An integration might remove repeated data entry. Compare these options before assuming a new platform is the answer.</p><h2>Know what makes the process different</h2><p>Bespoke software becomes more interesting when your workflow is specific to your business, available products leave important gaps, or several systems need to work together.</p><h2>Plan the first useful release</h2><p>Define one outcome you can evaluate. For a receiving tool, that might be recording discrepancies consistently and producing a clear history. Agree what is included, what is excluded and how you will test it.</p><h2>Consider the whole cost</h2><p>Development is one part. Hosting, support, migration, training, third-party services and future changes need a plan. Compare the full cost with configuring an existing product.</p><p>Explore our <a href="/consultancy">consultancy</a> and <a href="/software-development-northern-ireland">software development services</a>, or <a href="/contact">tell us what you have in mind</a>.</p><p class="article-byline">By <a href="https://www.glenadamson.co.uk/about">Glen Adamson</a>, founder of Modern Software.</p></article><CTA/></Base>
